{
 "principles": [
  {
   "title": "Build a balanced plate",
   "body": "About half vegetables and fruit, a quarter protein, and a quarter grains or starchy foods (whole grains when your stomach is comfortable with them), plus a little healthy fat."
  },
  {
   "title": "Include protein at every meal",
   "body": "It keeps you full and supports muscles: dal, beans, paneer, tofu, curd/yoghurt, eggs, fish, chicken, nuts and seeds."
  },
  {
   "title": "Choose mostly whole, minimally processed foods",
   "body": "Cook at home when you can."
  },
  {
   "title": "Go easy on added sugar",
   "body": "Fewer sweets, sugary drinks and packaged snacks. Fruit is the better sweet."
  },
  {
   "title": "Go easy on salt and deep-fried food",
   "body": "Flavour with herbs, spices, lemon, garlic and ginger instead."
  },
  {
   "title": "Use healthy fats in moderation",
   "body": "Nuts, seeds, avocado, and oils like mustard, groundnut or olive oil, rather than lots of butter, ghee or vanaspati."
  },
  {
   "title": "Drink water through the day",
   "body": "Let thirst and pale-yellow urine guide you. Plain water, buttermilk (chaas), unsweetened tea or infused water rather than sugary drinks."
  },
  {
   "title": "Eat regularly and mindfully",
   "body": "Sit down, slow down and stop when comfortably full."
  }
 ],
 "gentleGuidance": {
  "title": "Gentle on the stomach",
  "body": "Gentle meals use foods that are easier to digest: well-cooked, peeled and soft vegetables, refined grains like white rice, and lean proteins.\n\nA low-fibre diet is usually meant to be **short-term or guided by your doctor**. Fibre is important for long-term gut and heart health. When you feel better, try adding fibre back slowly, one food at a time, in small amounts.\n\n**See a doctor if** you have stomach pain that doesn't go away, blood in your stool, black stools, unexplained weight loss, vomiting, diarrhoea lasting more than a few days, difficulty swallowing, or symptoms that wake you at night."
 },
